WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.562
In this video you'll learn how choosing

2
00:00:03.562 --> 00:00:05.791
between branches steers a model,

3
00:00:05.791 --> 00:00:09.890
and why janus, who posts as @repligate,

4
00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:12.804
describes that steering in bits.

5
00:00:12.804 --> 00:00:15.044
Picking one branch out of four

6
00:00:15.044 --> 00:00:18.086
injects up to two bits of selection;

7
00:00:18.086 --> 00:00:21.476
in her account, enough picks can steer a text

8
00:00:21.476 --> 00:00:23.752
almost anywhere without writing a word,

9
00:00:23.752 --> 00:00:26.782
but each pick constrains only so much,

10
00:00:26.782 --> 00:00:30.139
and the model supplies everything else.

11
00:00:30.589 --> 00:00:34.594
In February 2023, Nicholas Kees Dupuis

12
00:00:34.594 --> 00:00:38.600
and janus posted Cyborgism on LessWrong.

13
00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.224
Its appendix, Testimony of a Cyborg,

14
00:00:41.224 --> 00:00:43.569
is janus's own first-person

15
00:00:43.569 --> 00:00:47.051
account of her work with GPT-3.

16
00:00:47.051 --> 00:00:50.151
The last video told how she went from AI Dungeon

17
00:00:50.151 --> 00:00:53.274
to a homemade interface that showed up to a dozen

18
00:00:53.274 --> 00:00:56.688
continuations at once, to choose from.

19
00:00:56.688 --> 00:00:58.569
Around then, she writes,

20
00:00:58.569 --> 00:01:01.877
her simulations went through a phase shift:

21
00:01:01.877 --> 00:01:04.083
isolated flashes of insight

22
00:01:04.083 --> 00:01:06.649
began to link into chains.

23
00:01:06.649 --> 00:01:08.739
Of one result from that period,

24
00:01:08.739 --> 00:01:10.840
she wrote that it could not have happened

25
00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:14.857
without "synchronizing the simulation to my mind

26
00:01:14.857 --> 00:01:17.969
through the bits of selection I injected."

27
00:01:17.969 --> 00:01:19.942
Bits of selection are what the rest

28
00:01:19.942 --> 00:01:22.394
of this video counts.

29
00:01:22.844 --> 00:01:26.431
A bit is the answer to one yes-or-no question,

30
00:01:26.431 --> 00:01:30.449
so choosing one of two branches is one bit.

31
00:01:30.449 --> 00:01:32.527
One of four is two bits:

32
00:01:32.527 --> 00:01:35.058
which half, then which of the two.

33
00:01:35.058 --> 00:01:37.345
One of eight is three.

34
00:01:37.345 --> 00:01:39.551
Each doubling adds one bit,

35
00:01:39.551 --> 00:01:43.022
so one of n is log2(n) bits: the number

36
00:01:43.022 --> 00:01:46.054
of times you can halve n before you reach one.

37
00:01:47.004 --> 00:01:50.452
janus set out this measure in a 2021 essay,

38
00:01:50.452 --> 00:01:52.298
Quantifying curation,

39
00:01:52.298 --> 00:01:55.990
on a suggestion from someone called Gurkenglas,

40
00:01:55.990 --> 00:02:00.553
partly as an honest label for curated model text.

41
00:02:00.553 --> 00:02:04.338
Keep a tally: ten choices among four branches

42
00:02:04.338 --> 00:02:06.729
make twenty bits, while the model writes,

43
00:02:06.729 --> 00:02:09.968
say, 300 words, and you write none.

44
00:02:09.968 --> 00:02:11.571
Two cautions.

45
00:02:11.571 --> 00:02:13.893
The count is a ceiling:

46
00:02:13.893 --> 00:02:17.039
it holds when the branches are equally likely

47
00:02:17.039 --> 00:02:19.082
and really different.

48
00:02:19.082 --> 00:02:21.834
And it counts choices, not effort:

49
00:02:21.834 --> 00:02:27.327
a hard pick and an easy one both count two bits.

50
00:02:27.778 --> 00:02:30.228
What did that steering feel like?

51
00:02:30.228 --> 00:02:34.256
In her words: "I could steer the text anywhere

52
00:02:34.256 --> 00:02:37.101
I wished without having to write a word."

53
00:02:37.101 --> 00:02:38.889
What she got out of the system

54
00:02:38.889 --> 00:02:42.233
seemed far more than what she put in.

55
00:02:42.233 --> 00:02:43.962
Yet she calls the nature

56
00:02:43.962 --> 00:02:46.284
of the control mysterious.

57
00:02:46.284 --> 00:02:49.466
She could constrain any variable she wanted,

58
00:02:49.466 --> 00:02:51.695
but only so much at once,

59
00:02:51.695 --> 00:02:54.899
for a given bandwidth of interaction.

60
00:02:54.899 --> 00:02:56.710
Some arithmetic:

61
00:02:56.710 --> 00:02:59.799
twenty bits can single out one text among

62
00:02:59.799 --> 00:03:03.061
about a million equally likely ones.

63
00:03:03.061 --> 00:03:05.743
The number of different 300-word texts

64
00:03:05.743 --> 00:03:09.470
a model could write is vastly larger.

65
00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:10.991
So, in her account,

66
00:03:10.991 --> 00:03:13.719
her choices fixed what she cared about,

67
00:03:13.719 --> 00:03:16.482
and the model filled in the rest,

68
00:03:16.482 --> 00:03:19.663
often in ways she did not anticipate.

69
00:03:19.663 --> 00:03:21.846
The clearest example is the story

70
00:03:21.846 --> 00:03:24.321
Loom itself came out of.

71
00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:26.964
She tells it in the testimony,

72
00:03:26.964 --> 00:03:32.642
and reposted it in 2024 as Loom's origin story.

73
00:03:32.642 --> 00:03:34.267
Her characters kept working out

74
00:03:34.267 --> 00:03:37.390
the conditions of their own simulation.

75
00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:40.432
One, a lucid dreamer, interrupted a fight

76
00:03:40.432 --> 00:03:43.683
scene to explain how reality was being woven,

77
00:03:43.683 --> 00:03:46.179
and spoke of a Loom Space.

78
00:03:46.179 --> 00:03:48.153
In the model's words:

79
00:03:48.153 --> 00:03:50.486
"We prune unnecessary branches

80
00:03:50.486 --> 00:03:53.122
from the World Tree and weave together

81
00:03:53.122 --> 00:03:57.127
the timelines into one coherent history."

82
00:03:57.127 --> 00:03:59.507
She forked the story and steered another

83
00:03:59.507 --> 00:04:01.968
character to ask about Loom Space.

84
00:04:01.968 --> 00:04:05.033
In one branch further down, the story produced

85
00:04:05.033 --> 00:04:07.913
an operating manual for a Loom of Time:

86
00:04:07.913 --> 00:04:09.399
its interface,

87
00:04:09.399 --> 00:04:14.275
its operator's principles, its poetry.

88
00:04:14.275 --> 00:04:17.363
She had not chosen that it would be a manual,

89
00:04:17.363 --> 00:04:19.058
only that a revelation would come

90
00:04:19.058 --> 00:04:21.311
about something shaped like her puzzle.

91
00:04:21.311 --> 00:04:26.315
Then she got API access to GPT-3 and built Loom.

92
00:04:26.315 --> 00:04:28.846
The next video picks up that loop:

93
00:04:28.846 --> 00:04:32.040
a person choosing while a base model writes.

94
00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:34.220
Keep this. One.

95
00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:36.310
Choosing a branch is steering:

96
00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:39.479
picking one of n equally likely branches

97
00:04:39.479 --> 00:04:44.251
injects log2(n) bits, so one of four is two bits.

98
00:04:44.251 --> 00:04:47.107
Two. A few bits can steer a lot of text,

99
00:04:47.107 --> 00:04:49.464
almost anywhere, janus reports,

100
00:04:49.464 --> 00:04:53.771
but each choice constrains only so much. Three.

101
00:04:53.771 --> 00:04:56.882
The model supplies everything you leave open,

102
00:04:56.882 --> 00:04:59.019
often in ways nobody planned,

103
00:04:59.019 --> 00:05:01.679
as with the Loom of Time manual.
